{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:41571662","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":8,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"41571662","source":"MED","pmid":"41571662","pmcid":"PMC12923738","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12923738"]},"doi":"10.1038/s41467-026-68801-w","title":"Comparative multi-omic analysis reveals conserved and derived mechanisms of fin and limb regeneration.","authorString":"F Sousa J, Lima G, Perez L, Schof H, Schneider I.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"F Sousa J","lastName":"F Sousa","firstName":"Josane","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences, Louisiana State University, Baton Rouge, LA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Lima G","lastName":"Lima","firstName":"Gabriela","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences, Louisiana State University, Baton Rouge, LA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Perez L","lastName":"Perez","firstName":"Louise","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-0468-0995"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences, Louisiana State University, Baton Rouge, LA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Schof H","lastName":"Schof","firstName":"Hannah","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-5859-8534"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences, Louisiana State University, Baton Rouge, LA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Schneider I","lastName":"Schneider","firstName":"Igor","initials":"I","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9046-7338"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences, Louisiana State University, Baton Rouge, LA, USA. igors@lsu.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7932-9818"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9046-7338"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-0468-0995"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-5859-8534"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data","related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"17","journalIssueId":4085585,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Nat Commun","title":"Nature communications","essn":"2041-1723","issn":"2041-1723","nlmid":"101528555","isoabbreviation":"Nat Commun"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1922","abstractText":"Comparative studies of vertebrate appendages offer a powerful framework for uncovering shared components of an ancestral regeneration toolkit. Here, we employed a multi-omics comparative approach leveraging the regenerative capacity of the axolotl, zebrafish, and Polypterus senegalus, a fish capable of full fin regeneration. We identified conserved markers of proximal and distal blastema territories, shared activation of DNA damage repair, hif1a-mediated hypoxia response, and sequential activation of pro- and anti-inflammatory program. Apical epithelial ridge markers were expressed in both the wound epidermis and distal mesenchyme during limb and fin regeneration. Notably, hif4a-expressing erythrocytes were uniquely associated with proximal limb and fin amputations but not fin rays, while epidermal myoglobin expression was upregulated only in Polypterus and zebrafish fins. Genome-wide chromatin profiling identified candidate regeneration-responsive elements and a conserved enrichment for AP-1 transcription factor binding. Together, these findings identify shared and derived mechanisms of limb and fin regeneration.","affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences, Louisiana State University, Baton Rouge, LA, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Comparative Study","research-article","Journal Ar